#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111539} :activity/id #uuid "91d80949-4bd6-531f-a855-c98a58ea2f32" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048764 :topic/id #uuid "bce11f5d-4b21-5819-b55b-282994760aee" :topic/math-academy-id 1511} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111922 :tutorial/content "Two angles are **supplementary** if their measures add up to $180^\\circ.$\n\n![](images/88/886f1ce19ad595b470d5ad13943b780bd5a22ee737908d9554e97bd08c9ce985.png)\n\nThese angles are supplementary because the sum of their measures is $180^\\circ{:}$\n\n$$\n105^\\circ +75^\\circ = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nA **linear pair** is a pair of adjacent angles formed when two lines intersect. The angles shown above form a linear pair.\n\nThe **linear pair postulate** states the following:\n\nIf two angles form a linear pair, then they are supplementary.\n\nThe converse is *not* true. That is, if two angles are supplementary, they do not necessarily form a linear pair.\n\nTo see why, consider the diagram shown below:\n\n![](images/29/29ff0dfefb0b535342f4e8060b37fb2404983fc150681bb02d18fa6e5b00f899.png)\n\nAlthough the angles are supplementary (the sum of their measures is $120^\\circ + 60^\\circ = 180^\\circ$), they do not form a linear pair of angles since they are not adjacent." :tutorial/id #uuid "ea9626ef-f56f-5668-8f47-81a9f68c9db4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9518 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "34d571bc-0e65-56b1-80d0-82208a4c2869" :step/math-academy-id 193869 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111540}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110627 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110357 :question/id #uuid "4ebed10c-0a87-528a-ba18-67d960ba7692" :question/math-academy-id "e-15641"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "931f7270-ec55-5c42-8c1b-6c7988f6c921" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048723} {:db/